The Pressure Cook and Steam Smart Programs are pressure cooking programs. Using pressurized steam ensures your dish is cooked evenly and deeply, for predictably delicious results every time.  The pot features 2 pressure levels to choose from:

<PIC:Manual30_20>
{{image description: Pressure level table: Low pressure 5.0-8.0 psi (35-55 kPa) for fish, seafood, soft vegetables, and rice; High pressure 9.4-12.3 psi (65-85 kPa) for eggs, meat, poultry, root vegetables, oats, beans, grains, bone broth, stew, and chili.}}

<PIC:Manual30_21>
{{image description: Minimum liquid required table listing 5.7 litres requiring 375 mL and 8 litres requiring 500 mL.}}

The Slow Cook Smart Program is comparable to the cooking process of traditional slow cookers, and is compatible for use with any of your slow cooker recipes. Follow recipe directions for slow cooking  The float valve should not rise during Slow Cook. The lid is removable throughout the cooking process for easy access. If the float valve rises, make sure the quick release button is set to the Vent position. Refer to Releasing pressure: Venting methods

<PIC:Manual30_22>
{{image description: Slow Cook temperature table. Low is for low and slow cooking with minimum 10 hours; High is faster. Notes mention using a vented lid and setting quick release to Vent.}}

Saute can be used in place of a frying pan, griddle or flat-top grill. Sauteing meat and vegetables before pressure cooking is the perfect way to boost flavors.

<PIC:Manual30_23>
{{image description: Saute temperature table. Low is for simmer, reduce, and caramelize; High is for pan sear, stir-fry, saute, and brown. Notes mention Hot display and a 30 minute maximum cooking time.}}

Sous Vide cooking involves cooking food underwater, in an air-tight, food-safe bag for a long period of time. Food cooks in its own juices and comes out delicious and unbelievably tender

